                         HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “Ven,” said Doña Luz, into his shoulder. Come. “Ven, Mikey. Ya.”
   I’m not going to tell you what he said. I couldn’t hear it. The horns
had begun again, and the drums, and a little girl in the second row was
banging a spoon on a pot, and a hundred people had stood up out of
their chairs. I know only that his shoulders came down, and that his
face, which I had known for twenty years, did a thing I hadn’t seen it
do since we were nine.
   It was loud.



The room didn’t shine. There was no light, no wind, no sudden thun‐
der. It was a drill hall with a very big roof, and some folding chairs,
and a lot of people on their feet, and a Rara procession going through
the middle of it like a river through a field.
   The local pull was gone.
   I felt it go. The pressed place under the sound lifted. A man near the
rail coughed and laughed at himself. Somebody’s phone rang in a
pocket, and nobody shushed it.
   On the Garden, the colors that had been draining toward the plat‐
form all night ran back out to the edges of the screen and kept going,
off the screen, as if they knew where they lived.
   Doña Fela was on her feet. So were Papo and Wilson. Don Chino’s
wife was dancing, very slightly, with her hands at her sides. Teodoro
had climbed on a chair and was reading his poem about the parking
ticket to the horns, who were, for once, a satisfactory audience.
   Sofía had a phone up in each hand, filming the hall in a slow arc,
and two more rubber-banded to the platform rail behind her, aimed at
the doors. Her mouth was moving: the exit, the exit, the curtain, the latch.
Vanessa stood at the north door with a chair in her hands, holding it
open with one foot, speaking quickly and clearly to the venue manager,
who had gone the color of cheese. Mr. Rodríguez, in his yellow vest,
was writing in his clipboard as fast as his hand would go. Desmond
stood in the doorway like a part of the building. Pri was passing out
water bottles. Yenni was counting heads.




